WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.600
Sylhet, Bangladesh. June, twenty twenty-two.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.213
Seven point two million people were caught by the flood. Many were never warned.

3
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:20.002
That week's flood maps used radar that stops at the leaves. Under the forest, the water stayed invisible.

4
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.016
Then NASA and ISRO launched NISAR. Every twelve days, it sees through cloud.

5
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.292
Back to the same forest, June twenty twenty-six. From space, a camera sees only cloud.

6
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.010
Ordinary radar finds the rivers. Not much more.

7
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:42.414
NISAR reaches the water beneath. Twenty-six square kilometres. Eleven that other radar missed.

8
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.785
Its waves slip past the leaves, strike the water and the trunks, and bounce straight back.

9
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.134
About three hundred and twenty thousand people live within a kilometre of it.

10
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.599
It counts the days too. Here, up to a hundred and eight.

11
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.414
For each village, one picture. Where the water lies, and when NISAR comes back.

12
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:11.414
Twenty-seven flooded forests, worldwide. In Mozambique, it mapped a flood the clouds hid from every camera.

13
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:17.032
Through smoke, it mapped nearly nineteen hundred square kilometres of burned Canadian forest.

14
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.146
Ground that moves. Seventy centimetres in Venezuela.

15
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.360
Thirty centimetres a year under Mexico City.

16
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.610
And ice. Greenland's fastest glacier flows up to thirty-six metres a day.

17
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.704
Every result is checked against something independent.

18
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:42.005
Where it can't see, it says so. And it is not a warning system.

19
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.276
For relief teams. Which villages need boats this week.

20
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.669
For communities, in their own language.

21
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.894
For scientists. Move the thresholds. Take the data.

22
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.909
For climate modellers. How long the wetlands stay wet.

23
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.899
For classrooms. A notebook for every place, and a game.

24
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.606
And for planners. Four changes, one method, evidence first.

25
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:14.613
All of it free and open. It works offline, in three languages.

26
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:22.501
Next. Every flooded forest on Earth, more radars passing more often, and river outlooks two weeks ahead.

27
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.432
Under the Canopy. What NISAR's radar sees through.

28
00:02:27.632 --> 00:02:29.400
